status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,709,2218,759],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0006","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":6,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"although these are less knowable, and less real, in themselves, they are what is accessible to us.","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,766,2146,817],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0007","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":7,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"Our goal is not to add yet another opinion to the disputes about non-manifest οὐσίαι, but to","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,824,2226,875],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0008","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":8,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"resolve the disputes; and we can do this only if we begin with the οὐσίαι we already know, and","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,881,2178,932],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"main","content_class":"main_text","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[{"bbox":null,"end_char":77,"semantic_role":"footnote_marker","span_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0009-visual-qa-marker-00","start_char":75,"styles":["superscript"],"target_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","text":"33"}],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0009","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":9,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"investigate whether they entail the existence of other prior οὐσίαι or not.33 Now Aristotle has","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,985,2241,1035],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[{"bbox":null,"end_char":2,"semantic_role":"none","span_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0010-visual-qa-marker-00","start_char":0,"styles":["superscript"],"target_id":null,"text":"32"}],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0010","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-32","paragraph_role":"start","reading_order":10,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"32 I have turned several plurals into singulars for ease of translation. note on transposition-question, cite Bonitz [who","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1041,2232,1083],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0011","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-32","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":11,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"cites Spengel]. the transposition is better described as moving two lines than as moving ten; there is no justification","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1089,2149,1130],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0012","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-32","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":12,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"for Jaeger's claim that the ten-line section is a loose tag, inserted later and in not quite the right place. note van","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1137,2184,1178],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0013","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-32","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":13,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"Arnim's alternative. Irwin and now Code-Most-Laks seem to want to keep the manuscript order, which I think is","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1184,2217,1226],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0014","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-32","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":14,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"simply impossible, because of the need for an antecedent for αὐτοῦ in 1029b13 (the particle καί in this line would","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1232,2171,1274],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0015","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-32","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":15,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"also be wrong; it would have to be δέ or the like, see Sicking and van Ophuijsen for the difference). not that the","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1281,2172,1322],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0016","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-32","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":16,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"consequences of going back to the manuscript order would be as cosmic as some people seem to think; see note","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1329,412,1370],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0017","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-32","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":17,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"below","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1369,2233,1418],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[{"bbox":null,"end_char":2,"semantic_role":"none","span_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0018-visual-qa-marker-00","start_char":0,"styles":["superscript"],"target_id":null,"text":"33"}],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0018","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"start","reading_order":18,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"33 The point of the ethical comparison is as follows. Aristotle is taking up a point which had originally been made in","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1424,2139,1466],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0019","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":19,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"a medical or dietetic context and had been extended metaphorically to ethics or politics, and he is extending it","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1472,2245,1514],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0020","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":20,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"further to epistemology. Someone who is sick or constitutionally weak should not be given the hard-to-digest foods,","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1520,2121,1562],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0021","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":21,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"such as beef, that would be appropriate for a healthy person or especially for a person preparing for vigorous","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1568,2258,1610],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0022","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":22,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"physical activity (like Polydamas the pancratiast, Republic I 338c; on dietetic warnings against over-strong foods cp.","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1616,2220,1658],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0023","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":23,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"also the On Ancient Medicine). So to make this person healthy and vigorous, we should not give him what is good","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1664,2225,1705],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0024","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":24,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"for a person in good condition, but rather what is good for this person; and we hope, beginning by giving him what","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1712,2236,1753],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0025","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":25,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"is good for him, to bring him to a good condition, i.e. to a condition where he will be benefitted by what is good for","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1760,2255,1801],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0026","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":26,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"a person in good condition. Now there is an apparent disagreement between what appears good to the healthy person","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1808,2249,1849],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0027","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":27,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"and what appears good to the sick person. Protagoras, as represented in the Theaetetus, had used this as an argument","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1855,2151,1897],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0028","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":28,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"for relativism: there is nothing that is good ἁπλῶς, there is only the good-for-this-person and the good-for-that-","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1904,2214,1945],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0029","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":29,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"person; the doctor, in transforming the patient, is not giving him truer perceptions of what is good, but only better,","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1952,2231,1993],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0030","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":30,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"i.e. more agreeable perceptions. This chimes with Protagoras' attitude toward legislation: there is no such thing as a","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,1999,2155,2041],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0031","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":31,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"good law ἁπλῶς, only a law that is good for this particular constitution, that is, a law that tends to preserve this","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2047,2256,2089],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0032","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":32,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"particular constitution; the legislative advisor, who is analogous to the doctor, does not propose a law that more truly","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2095,2254,2137],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0033","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":33,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"describes what things are right and wrong, but only a law that will have better results for this constitution, i.e. results","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2143,2227,2185],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0034","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":34,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"that tend to preserve it and will be perceived as better by its rulers. Aristotle is aware of Protagoras' attitude in both","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2191,2217,2233],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0035","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":35,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"the medical and the political cases, and rejects his relativism in both cases. What is good ἁπλῶς is not (against e.g.","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2239,2117,2281],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0036","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":36,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"the Euthydemus) what always benefits, which is too much to expect, but just what benefits someone in good","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2287,2204,2328],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0037","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":37,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"condition. Some laws are objectively just, and they are the ones that are beneficial to, i.e. tend to preserve, a good","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2335,2254,2376],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0038","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":38,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"constitution rather than a perverted constitution. And we need not conclude (with the Euthydemus) that wealth is not","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2383,2260,2424],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0039","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":39,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"good, because it is likely to harm someone who is in a bad moral condition and will therefore use the money badly; a","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2431,2255,2472],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0040","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":40,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"virtuous person will use the money to carry out virtuous activities and thus will be benefitted by it, so wealth is good","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2478,2242,2520],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0041","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":41,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"for a person in good condition and is therefore good ἁπλῶς. But obviously we should not give unlimited money to a","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2527,2216,2568],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0042","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":42,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"child, and we should be wary of wishing unlimited money for ourselves in our present condition. In the passage of","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2574,2246,2616],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0043","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":43,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"the Ethics (EE IV,1 = NE V,1 1129b1-6) which Ross rightly cites to explain the ἐν ταῖς πράξεσι comparison of Z3,","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2622,2225,2664],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0044","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":44,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"Aristotle, drawing on a religious-moralizing commonplace (cp. Plato Laws III 687-8, VII 801), says that people do","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2670,2237,2712],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0045","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":45,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"wrong in praying for the goods of fortune, when it may turn out badly for them if their prayers are answered: \"these","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2718,2226,2760],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0046","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":46,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"things are always ἁπλῶς good, but not always good for a particular person. People pray for these things and pursue","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2766,2157,2808],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"mixed","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0047","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":47,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"them, but  they should not; rather, they should pray that the things that are good ἁπλῶς should also be good for","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2814,2193,2856],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0048","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":48,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"themselves, but choose the things that are good for themselves.\" So we should, for now, avoid excessive external","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2862,2208,2904],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0049","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":49,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"goods, pursuing moderate external goods and cultivating our soul, hoping to become such virtuous people that we","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2910,2206,2951],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0050","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":50,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"will deserve, and be able to make good use of, great external goods at some point. This is the idea that Aristotle is","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[300,2958,2204,2999],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"notes","content_class":"footnote","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"eng","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0051","note_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-note-33","paragraph_role":"continuation","reading_order":51,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"now extending to epistemology. Someone who is in good cognitive condition finds most knowable the things that","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"},{"bbox":[1250,3073,1300,3110],"canonical_reference":null,"column_id":"furniture","content_class":"page_number","geometry_status":"human_verified","hierarchy_level":null,"inline_spans":[],"language":"zxx","leaf_id":"single","line_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522-native-0052","note_id":null,"paragraph_role":"not_applicable","reading_order":52,"source":"human","structure_status":"human_verified","text":"514","transcription_status":"human_verified","typography_status":"human_verified"}],"page_id":"menn-metaphysics-pdf522","schema":"mpdf-closed-world-ocr-gold-page","schema_version":"1.2"}
